1e1524551de5b82549a7cc260e5d2acce74167ffe319d2dc6e94"},"seconds":12.51579737663269,"seed":1147403929563265339,"stop":"eos","text":"First, consider the last digit of \\(1001001 \\times 1000100\\):\n\\[\n1 \\times 0 = 0.\n\\]\nSo, the last digit of this product is 0.\n\nNext, consider the last digit of \\(989899 \\times 11102\\):\n\\[\n9 \\times 2 = 18.\n\\]\nSo, the last digit of this product is 8. Since the first term ends in 0, the last non-zero digit of the sum is the last digit of the second term, which is 8. The first term contributes trailing zeros, so the rightmost non-zero digit of the entire expression is 8, and it is followed by 6 zeros (from the first term).\n\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(8, 6)}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{(8, 6)}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the rightmost non-zero digit in the expression \\((1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001)\\), we will simplify and analyze the expression step by step.\n\nFirst, let's rewrite the expression in a more convenient form:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101) + (989899)(1001001) - (1001)(989899) - (1010101)(1001).\n\\]\nWe can factor out common terms:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1010101 - 1001) + (989899)(1001001 - 989899).\n\\]\nSimplifying the terms inside the parentheses:\n\\[\n1010101 - 1001 = 1000100 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 1001001 - 989899 = 11102.\n\\]\nSo the expression becomes:\n\\[\n(1001001)(1000100) + (989899)(11102).\n\\]\nNow, we need to find the rightmost non-zero digit of this expression. We will do this by considering the last digits of each term and their product.\n\nFirst, consider the last digit of \\(1001001 \\times 1000100\\):\n\\[\n1 \\times 0 = 0.\n\\]\nSo, the last digit of this product is 0.\n\nNext, consider the last digit of \\(989899 \\times 11102\\):\n\\[\n9 \\times 2 = 18.\n\\]\nSo, the last digit of this product is 8. Since the first term ends in 0, the last non-zero digit of the sum is the last digit of the second term, which is 8. The first term contributes trailing zeros, so the rightmost non-zero digit of the entire expression is 8, and it is followed by 6 zeros (from the first term).\n\nThus, the ordered pair \\((a, b)\\) is \\(\\boxed{(8, 6)}\\).","id":"algebra:117"}
